.Dd October 2, 2026
.Dt SEND 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm send ,
.Nm sendto ,
.Nm sendmsg ,
.Nm sendmmsg
.Nd send data on a socket
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn send "int fd" "const void *buf" "size_t len" "int flags"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn sendto "int fd" "const void *buf" "size_t len" "int flags" "const struct sockaddr *addr" "socklen_t addrlen"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn sendmsg "int fd" "const struct msghdr *msg" "int flags"
.Pp
.Fd #define _GNU_SOURCE
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fn sendmmsg "int fd" "struct mmsghdr *msgvec" "unsigned int vlen" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions send
.Fa len
bytes from
.Fa buf
on socket
.Fa fd .
Musl implements
.Fn send
as
.Fn sendto
with a null destination and zero address length.
Both therefore use subcall 11 of i386
.Xr socketcall 2 ,
system call 102.
The separate kernel
.Nm send
operation, subcall 9, which glibc's
.Fn send
makes, sends on any socket: a stream to its peer, and any other socket as
.Fn sendto
with no destination sends.
.Fn sendmsg ,
subcall 16 or the direct call, number 370, sends the buffers of
.Fa msg
in order, to
.Fa msg_name
when it is set: a stream sends them one after another and stops at the
first that goes short; a UDP socket gathers up to eight into one
datagram; a raw or packet socket takes one buffer, and more fail with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
Control messages of an IPv4 socket are ignored.
On an
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket all three calls carry what
.Xr unix 7
describes, control messages included.
The direct calls
.Fn sendto ,
number 369, and
.Fn sendmsg
take their arguments in registers.
.Pp
.Fn sendmmsg ,
subcall 20 and the direct call 345, sends each of the
.Fa vlen
messages
.Fa msgvec
describes, at most 1024, as
.Fn sendmsg
sends one, and stores each message's count in its
.Va msg_len .
It returns the number of messages sent, or the first error when none was;
GNU libc's resolver sends its two queries with it.
.Pa tests/musl/callsmusl.c
sends two datagrams in one call and receives them in one
.Xr recvmmsg 2 .
.Ss Streams
This section describes TCP streams;
.Xr unix 7
describes
.Dv AF_UNIX
ones.
A stream sends to its connected peer.
.Fn sendto
ignores
.Fa addr
and
.Fa addrlen
for a stream.
A send copies as many bytes as fit into the send buffer and may return
a short count.
When the buffer is full, a nonzero send waits for space unless
.Dv MSG_DONTWAIT
is set in
.Fa flags
or the descriptor has
.Dv O_NONBLOCK .
Other flag bits are ignored.
A signal can interrupt the wait.
.Pp
A stream must be in the established or close-wait state.
If it cannot send, a pending TCP error is returned and cleared.
If the stream is outside those states and has no pending TCP error, the
call fails with
.Er EPIPE .
A TCP send generates no
.Dv SIGPIPE .
An
.Dv AF_UNIX
stream or sequenced-packet send that fails with
.Er EPIPE ,
its peer gone or the connection shut down in its direction, posts
.Dv SIGPIPE
to the sending thread, whose default action ends the process, unless
.Fa flags
has
.Dv MSG_NOSIGNAL ,
as in Linux.
.Ss Datagrams
For an IPv4 datagram socket,
.Fa addr
specifies the destination address and port in a
.Vt sockaddr_in .
If the destination pointer is null or
.Fa addrlen
is zero, the socket must have a peer set by
.Xr connect 2 .
An explicit destination overrides that peer for this send.
A supplied address must contain at least its first eight bytes.
The port and address are in network byte order.
Destinations in 127/8 are replaced by the kernel's local IPv4 address.
.Pp
An unbound socket is first assigned an ephemeral source port.
A datagram may contain at most 1472 bytes; an empty datagram is valid.
The limited broadcast and subnet broadcast addresses require
.Dv SO_BROADCAST .
A subnet broadcast leaves by the interface on that subnet, with that
interface's address as its source; the limited broadcast,
255.255.255.255, leaves by the first interface.
Flags do not affect datagram sends.
.Pp
The stack does not fragment outgoing datagrams.
An unresolved next-hop hardware address causes an ARP request and the
current datagram is discarded, although the send reports success.
.Ss Raw and packet sockets
Flags do not affect raw or packet sends.
A raw ICMP send requires an explicit IPv4 destination.
The caller supplies the ICMP message, including its checksum; the
kernel adds the IPv4 header and uses the socket's
.Dv IP_TTL .
The ICMP message may contain at most 1480 bytes, leaving room for the
20-byte IPv4 header within the 1500-byte MTU.
Larger messages return
.Er EMSGSIZE
before routing or queueing, including on loopback.
An ioctl-only raw socket cannot send.
.Pp
An
.Dv AF_PACKET
datagram send requires a 20-byte
.Vt sockaddr_ll
with a six-byte destination hardware address.
The protocol must be IPv4 or ARP; a zero protocol uses the socket's
protocol.
A nonzero interface index selects a registered interface.
Index zero uses the socket's bound interface, or the first interface
when none is bound.
The payload must contain 1 through 1500 bytes.
The kernel adds the Ethernet header.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success,
.Fn send ,
.Fn sendto
and
.Fn sendmsg
return the number of bytes accepted, and
.Fn sendmmsg
the number of messages sent, with each message's count of bytes in its
.Va msg_len .
A stream may return fewer bytes than requested.
A successful datagram send returns
.Fa len .
Success does not establish delivery to the peer.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/loopprobe.c
binds a UDP socket, sends a message to that port at 127.0.0.1 with
subcall 11, then receives and compares the payload.
It also checks an empty datagram.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EDESTADDRREQ]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
A UDP broadcast was attempted without
.Dv SO_BROADCAST .
.It Bq Er EAFNOSUPPORT
A raw ICMP destination has an address family other than
.Dv AF_INET .
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
A nonblocking stream send found no buffer space, or an ephemeral UDP
port could not be assigned.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er ECONNREFUSED
A pending TCP error reports that the connection was refused, or the peer of
a connected datagram socket answered an earlier datagram with a port
unreachable.
.It Bq Er ECONNRESET
A pending TCP error reports that the connection was reset.
.It Bq Er EDESTADDRREQ
An unconnected UDP socket was given no destination, or a raw ICMP
socket was given no destination or fewer than eight address bytes.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The payload or required address cannot be read, the length exceeds
the signed 32-bit range, or the
.Fn socketcall
argument array cannot be read.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal interrupted a stream send waiting for buffer space.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
A supplied UDP address is shorter than eight bytes, a packet address
is shorter than 20 bytes, its hardware-address length is not six, or
its effective protocol is neither IPv4 nor ARP.
.It Bq Er EMSGSIZE
A UDP payload exceeds 1472 bytes, a raw ICMP message exceeds 1480 bytes,
or a packet payload is empty or exceeds 1500 bytes.
.It Bq Er ENETDOWN
A packet send has no interface through which to transmit.
.It Bq Er ENETRESET
A pending TCP error reports that a local address change ended the
connection.
.It Bq Er ENETUNREACH
No route exists for an IPv4 datagram.
.It Bq Er ENOBUFS
The local IPv4 delivery queue is full or the link layer cannot accept
an IPv4 datagram.
.It Bq Er ENODEV
A packet destination specifies an unregistered interface index.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa fd
does not refer to a socket.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
The socket is ioctl-only, or a raw ICMP send requested a broadcast
destination.
.It Bq Er EPIPE
A stream is not in a sending state and has no pending TCP error.
.It Bq Er ETIMEDOUT
A pending TCP error reports that the connection timed out.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr recv 2 ,
.Xr setsockopt 2 ,
.Xr shutdown 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketcall 2
